## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical streaming analytics under fixed memory, focusing on real-time percentile estimation and how rebinding affects accuracy and cost.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms, fixed memory, boundary recomputation, percentile estimation, amortized analysis.
- Trade-offs between K, R, and accuracy; cache-friendly bucket access.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Histogram85 {
  constructor(M, K) {
    this.M = M; this.K = K;
    this.bounds = Array.from({length: K}, (_,i)=> Math.floor((i+1)*M/K));
    this.counts = new Array(K).fill(0);
  }
  update(x){
    let bucket = Math.min(this.K-1, Math.floor(x/ (this.M/this.K)));
    this.counts[bucket]++;
  }
  total(){ return this.counts.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0); }
  percentile(p){ // p in [0,1]
    const total = this.total();
    const target = p * total;
    let acc = 0;
    for(let i=this.K-1; i>=0; i--){
      acc += this.counts[i];
      if(acc >= target) return this.bounds[i-1] !== undefined ? this.bounds[i-1] : this.M;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose K and R for skewed data?
- How does this scale with M and batch size B?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests online order-statistics handling with batched updates and amortized reasoning; demonstrates a practical, provable structure.

## Key Concepts
- Order statistics with incremental data
- Amortized analysis over batches
- Two-heap data structure (k smallest vs the rest)
- Space vs time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class KthSmallest {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.L = new MaxHeap(); // holds k smallest
    this.R = new MinHeap(); // holds the rest
  }
  insertBatch(arr) {
    for (const x of arr) this.R.push(x);
    while (this.L.size() < this.k && !this.R.empty()) this.L.push(this.R.pop());
    while (!this.R.empty() && this.L.top > this.R.top) {
      const a = this.L.pop();
      const b = this.R.pop();
      this.L.push(b);
      this.R.push(a);
    }
  }
  kth() { return this.L.top; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How handle duplicates or dynamic k?  
- What are alternatives (order-statistics tree, Fenwick) and trade-offs?  
- How does complexity change if B ≫ k?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design a fixed-memory streaming algorithm for heavy hitter detection—a common requirement in real-world scenarios like analytics dashboards, network monitoring, and recommendation systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Misra-Gries algorithm**: A space-efficient algorithm for finding items that appear more than 1/m of the time
- **Heavy hitters**: Values that appear frequently in a stream (above a threshold)
- **Space-time tradeoffs**: Analyzing memory usage versus per-element update time

## Algorithm Analysis
| Metric | Complexity |
|--------|------------|
| Per-element update | O(1) |
| Per-batch query | O(m) = O(1) since m=2 |
| Memory usage | O(m) = O(2) = O(1) fixed |

## Code Example
```javascript
class MisraGries {
  constructor(m = 2) {
    this.m = m;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.m) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      for (const [key, val] of this.counters) {
        this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
        if (this.counters.get(key) === 0) this.counters.delete(key);
      }
    }
  }

  getHeavyHitters() {
    return Array.from(this.counters.entries())
      .filter(([_, count]) => count > 0)
      .map(([value, count]) => ({ value, count }));
  }
}
```

## Small Example (m=2, stream: 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2)
| Element | Counter State | Heavy Hitters |
|---------|---------------|---------------|
| 1 | {1:1} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:1, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 1 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 3 | {1:1, 2:0} → {1:1} | 1 |
| 1 | {1:2} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |

## Follow-up Questions
- **Sliding windows**: Use a timestamped variant or combine with a separate structure tracking element ages
- **Ties**: Resolve by recency (most recently incremented) or arbitrarily since counts are equal
- **Accuracy**: Misra-Gries guarantees all heavy hitters are found but may include false positives; lowering the threshold (increasing m) reduces false positives at higher memory cost

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental connectivity intuition, DSU basics, and amortized analysis for real-world batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Union-Find with path compression and union by rank
- Alpha(n)
- Batch vs online update trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n){
    this.parent = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.components = n;
  }
  find(x){ return this.parent[x]===x? x: this.find(this.parent[x]); }
  union(a,b){ let ra=this.find(a), rb=this.find(b); if(ra===rb) return false; if(this.rank[ra]<this.rank[rb])[ra,rb]=[rb,ra]; this.parent[rb]=ra; if(this.rank[ra]===this.rank[rb]) this.rank[ra]++; this.components--; return true; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for directed graphs and counting weak components?
- What heuristics decide when to trigger a rebuild versus continuing online?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests sliding-window frequency tracking with fixed memory and per-element updates, a common real-time pattern in fraud/rate-limiting use cases. It also shows practical data-structure trade-offs for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with fixed memory
- Circular buffer + hashmap for frequencies
- Per-element O(1) updates; batch O(B)
- Threshold tracking to avoid full scans
- Choosing W, r, B depends on latency and memory constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCounter {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.buf = new Array(W);
    this.head = 0;
    this.size = 0;
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.crossed = 0; // number of IDs currently crossing the threshold
  }
  processBatch(batch, r) {
    for (const id of batch) {
      // increment
      const c = this.freq.get(id) || 0;
      const nc = c + 1;
      this.freq.set(id, nc);
      if (nc === r) this.crossed++;
      // push to circular buffer
      const idx = (this.head + this.size) % this.W;
      this.buf[idx] = id;
      if (this.size < this.W) {
        this.size++;
      } else {
        // evict oldest
        const old = this.buf[this.head];
        this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
        const co = this.freq.get(old) || 0;
        const nco = co - 1;
        if (nco <= 0) this.freq.delete(old);
        else this.freq.set(old, nco);
        if (co >= r && nco < r) this.crossed--;
      }
    }
    return this.crossed > 0;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple thresholds or weighted events? 
- What are trade-offs vs Misra-Gries / Space-Saving for skewed data? 
- How would you parallelize across shards or handle bursts with variable B?

